    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d253">

                <pb facs="0272" n="268" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg268"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94119/443: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in the United
                        Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-08-20T14:00:00-04:00">August 20, 1938—2
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>466. Your 791, August 18, 2 p.m. With reference to the first sentence of your
                    telegram under reference you will note from the Department’s 443, August 12, 6
                        p.m.,<anchor n="70" xml:id="d253-f1"/> that the Secretary in his talk with
                    the Chinese Ambassador here<anchor n="71" xml:id="d253-f2"/> made no categorical
                    statement of the attitude of this Government such as appears from your telegram
                    to have been attributed to this Government by the British Foreign Office in the
                    information given the Chinese Ambassador at London; the Secretary merely posed a
                    question, offered for consideration general comments and indicated that
                    additional action on our part was not to be expected at this time. Your telegram
                    would appear to indicate that the information conveyed by the Foreign Office to
                    the Chinese Ambassador at London carried the implication that the <pb
                        facs="0273" n="269" xml:id="pg269"/> responsibility for a negative decision
                    on the question of good offices rested on this Government. While we would have
                    seen no objection to the British Foreign Office informing the Chinese Ambassador
                    that we had been consulted and that our views were as indicated by us, we feel
                    that the Foreign Office should not have left, if indeed they did leave, the
                    Chinese Ambassador in any doubt that the British Government’s decision was made
                    on its own initiative.</p>
                <p>Department desires that in your discretion you bring this point orally and
                    informally to the attention of the Foreign Office.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
